From f0e77e3a9e2d65170134bde2b5c4a76698c20765 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:03:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Deep colour above 340 MHz on i915 is checked on
 the display, and goes to 8 bits where it did not lock

A Tiger Lake laptop (HP ProBook 640 G8, port B, a native HDMI port of
a combo PHY) sent 3840x2160 at 24 Hz in RGB of 10 bits, 370.878 MHz,
scrambled, to an LG TV, which showed no picture. The same TV, on the
same input (the same EDID and the same CEC address), showed the same
link from a GeForce RTX 5050 (370.875 MHz, 10 bits, scrambled), and
from the same laptop 3840x2160 at 60 Hz in 8 bits (594 MHz, scrambled)
and 1920x1080 at 60 Hz in 10 bits (185.625 MHz). Every value was within
what the port (594 MHz for display version 12 and its VBT; i915 had set
594 MHz on it) and the display (600 MHz, 30-bit deep colour) declare:
what fails is deep colour above 340 MHz from that port, which nothing
in the EDID or the driver tells. The 10 bits come from Kodi's depth of
an SDR picture of more than 8 bits (Dolby Vision mapped to SDR), and
from LibreELEC's linux 9800, which keeps 12 bits off from display
version 11 on.

Where i915 or xe (Kodi sets "max bpc" on their HDMI connectors) sends
such a link with a mode set (a mode whose 10-bit TMDS clock is above
340 MHz while its 8-bit one is not: 3840x2160 at 24 to 30 Hz), the
display's SCDC is read 2.5 s later, on a thread of its own, through the
DDC adapter that the kernel names for the connector in sysfs
(cardN-<connector>/ddc), after its EDID is checked to be the
connector's: only reads, only that bus. Where the display says that it
did not lock (Status_Flags_0 with the clock but not every channel
locked, or TMDS_Config without the setup of the scrambled link), that
mode goes out at 8 bits for the rest of the session, which the port and
the display take below 340 MHz, and the output is set again at once;
kodi.log says why. Where it locked, nothing changes and kodi.log says
what it said, so that a display that locks and still shows nothing can
be told from one that does not lock.

The read of the SCDC through an adapter is the one of NVIDIA's ports
(ReadScdcOf()), split so that both use it.
